  "usedTryThisNowFrames": [
    "Before your next advice-giving conversation, write one sentence naming the fear or certainty you would hide behind your role, then enter the conversation without using that role as armor.",
    "Choose one stuck person you are trying to help and remove one covert demand from your next sentence, leaving only what you understand and what you can honestly offer.",
    "After a difficult exchange, ask what the other person could say by the end that they could not say at the start; if the answer is nothing, adjust your stance.",
    "Take one human situation you are judging by a metric and add one lived-experience question that the metric cannot answer but the person can.",
    "Name one sign that a person is less defended this week than last week without calling it success, failure, or a stage.",
    "In one role where you feel locked into an image, make one small truthful adjustment in what you say next, without announcing a new identity.",
    "In a scripted conversation, pause once to ask what is actually happening between you before moving to the next planned step.",
    "Look at one goal you inherited from approval, then rewrite it as a question about what would be more congruent today.",
    "When you reach for composure today, ask what real feeling or fact you are trying not to include, then include it in one sentence.",
    "Before accepting a human measurement as the whole story, write the one person-level question the method leaves outside its frame.",
    "Take one apparent improvement and ask what changed underneath: behavior, self-understanding, defensiveness, or merely the performance.",
    "For one helping method you trust, name what research can test and what the client must still be allowed to reveal from inside the experience.",
    "In one teaching moment, replace one explanation you planned to give with a question that requires the learner to locate their own stake.",
    "Pick one lesson or explanation and add the moment where the learner must connect it to a real decision they face.",
    "In your next group learning moment, leave one real choice with participants and watch how responsibility changes the room before you intervene.",
    "In one family conflict, say what you understand before stating what you want, and check whether the other person becomes more honest or more defended.",
    "In one charged disagreement, summarize the other position until they would recognize it, then ask what changed in the room before making your point.",
    "After one strained interaction, map what each person understood, concealed, and defended before deciding what to repair.",
    "Before judging a new idea, give it ninety seconds of description without evaluation and notice whether it becomes more specific or collapses.",
    "For one behavior-shaping system you use, name the benefit, the control point, and the person whose freedom is most at risk.",
    "Look at one policy, platform, or institution and ask whether it treats people as becoming persons or as units to steer."
  ],
